     2,
     "<div lang=\"EN-GB\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\"><div><div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Dear distance samplers<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">I concluded my analysis some ago, yet looking at some of the results now, I get slightly confused.<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">Especially regarding how to present my findings.<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div lang=\"EN-GB\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\"><div><div><div>\r\n<p><u></u><span>1.<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">     \r\n</span></span><u></u>Why does df for many of my results have two decimals? - do you just remove the decimals, rounding the number?<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:#1f497d\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n</div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div lang=\"EN-GB\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\"><div><div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">df are determined both by the number of lines/points in your design and by the number of detections.  A Satterthwaite approximation is used to calculate them,\r\n and it does not give an integer value.  Probably simpler to round for quoting results.\r\n<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div lang=\"EN-GB\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\"><div><div><div>\r\n<p><u></u><span>2.<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">     \r\n</span></span><u></u>The CV seems to be calculated form the SE. Is that statistically allowed? - when CV is usually calculated from SD<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:#1f497d\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n</div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div lang=\"EN-GB\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\"><div><div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">CV can be defined either way.  The one that makes sense in the distance sampling context is se/estimate.<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div lang=\"EN-GB\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\"><div><div><div>\r\n<p><u></u><span>3.<span style=\"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;\">     \r\n</span></span><u></u>Do you write your results as density +- SE and abundance +- SE? - or is it better to use the 95%CI in parenthesis after the results?<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:#1f497d\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n</div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div lang=\"EN-GB\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\"><div><div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">I would quote confidence limits – they’re more informative.  Whichever you use, make sure that it is clear to the reader what you are presenting.<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n</div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div lang=\"EN-GB\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\"><div><div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\">4. Do reviewers prefer to have encounter rate and such included in the results section, or are density and abundance results usually enough? - the article is not a methods article.<u></u><u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n</div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div lang=\"EN-GB\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\"><div><div><div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"color:#1f497d\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\">It really depends on the purpose of your analyses – and on what a referee feels is required!  Generally, it’s better to give more rather than less, unless it\r\n confuses the message.  It’s easier to remove detail later than to add detail.  Or you can put the details of the analysis into an appendix.<u></u><u></u></span></p>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif;color:#1f497d\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n"
